    <div class="prose-thai">
      <h2>Why Green Curry Is the Perfect Keto Meal</h2>
      <p>Thai green curry (gaeng keow wan) is the crown jewel of Thai cuisine — and it happens to be one of the most keto-friendly dishes you can make. The sauce is built on full-fat coconut milk, which is rich in MCTs that support ketosis. The green curry paste is made from fresh green chilies, lemongrass, galangal, and herbs — all virtually carb-free. And the protein and vegetables make it a complete, satisfying meal.</p>

      <p>A properly made keto green curry contains 8-12g net carbs per serving, with most of those carbs coming from the vegetables. It's the kind of meal that makes keto feel like a lifestyle, not a diet.</p>

      <h2>What Makes Green Curry "Green"</h2>
      <p>Unlike red curry (which uses dried red chilies), green curry gets its color and flavor from fresh green ingredients:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Green Thai chilies (prik chee fa):</strong> The primary chili. Milder than bird chilies but still packs heat.</li>
        <li><strong>Green bird chilies:</strong> For extra heat. Use sparingly.</li>
        <li><strong>Cilantro (roots, stems, and leaves):</strong> Adds freshness and herbal notes.</li>
        <li><strong>Kaffir lime zest:</strong> The aromatic citrus note that defines Thai cuisine.</li>
        <li><strong>Thai basil:</strong> The anise-like finish that makes green curry unmistakable.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Keto Thai Green Curry Recipe</h2>
      <h3>Ingredients (Serves 4)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>2 cans (13.5 oz each) full-fat coconut milk — <strong>do not use light</strong></li>
        <li>3-4 tbsp green curry paste (Maesri or Mae Ploy recommended)</li>
        <li>1.5 lbs boneless, skinless chicken thighs, cut into bite-sized pieces</li>
        <li>1 cup Thai eggplant, quartered (or Japanese eggplant)</li>
        <li>1 cup green beans, trimmed and halved</li>
        <li>1 cup bamboo shoots, drained and rinsed</li>
        <li>4-5 kaffir lime leaves, torn</li>
        <li>1 cup Thai basil leaves (or sweet basil in a pinch)</li>
        <li>2 tbsp fish sauce</li>
        <li>1-2 tsp erythritol or monk fruit sweetener</li>
        <li>2-3 Thai bird chilies, crushed (optional, for extra heat)</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Instructions</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Fry the paste (critical step):</strong> Open one can of coconut milk without shaking. Scoop the thick cream from the top (about 1/2 cup) into a wok or large pot. Heat over medium-high until the cream separates — you'll see the oil rise to the surface. This takes 3-4 minutes.</li>
        <li>Add the green curry paste to the hot coconut cream. Stir-fry for 2-3 minutes until the paste is very fragrant and the oil takes on a green tint. This step develops the flavor — don't rush it.</li>
        <li>Add the chicken and stir-fry in the paste until the outside is sealed (about 3 minutes). The chicken doesn't need to be cooked through yet.</li>
        <li>Pour in the remaining coconut milk (both cans). Add the kaffir lime leaves. Bring to a gentle simmer — not a boil.</li>
        <li>Add the eggplant, green beans, and bamboo shoots. Simmer for 15-20 minutes until the vegetables are tender and the chicken is cooked through. The sauce will thicken slightly.</li>
        <li>Season with fish sauce and sweetener. Taste and adjust — it should be salty, spicy, and just barely sweet. The sweetness should be subtle, not dominant.</li>
        <li>Remove from heat. Stir in the Thai basil leaves — they'll wilt in the residual heat.</li>
        <li>Serve immediately in bowls. Traditionally eaten without rice, but cauliflower rice is a great accompaniment.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Macros per serving:</strong> ~450 calories, 35g fat, 8g net carbs, 28g protein</p>

      <h2>Pro Tips for Restaurant-Quality Green Curry</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Don't shake the coconut milk:</strong> You want the cream to separate so you can fry the paste in it. This is the #1 secret to authentic Thai curry.</li>
        <li><strong>Fry the paste until you can smell it across the kitchen:</strong> Raw curry paste tastes flat. Fried curry paste is aromatic and complex.</li>
        <li><strong>Simmer, don't boil:</strong> Boiling coconut milk can cause it to curdle. Keep it at a gentle bubble.</li>
        <li><strong>Thai eggplant is worth finding:</strong> Regular eggplant works, but Thai eggplant (small, round, green-striped) has a firmer texture and slightly bitter flavor that's perfect for curry.</li>
        <li><strong>Make extra:</strong> Green curry tastes even better the next day. Make a double batch for easy keto meal prep.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Variations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Beef green curry:</strong> Use beef sirloin, sliced thin. Simmer 25-30 minutes until tender.</li>
        <li><strong>Seafood green curry:</strong> Use shrimp, scallops, and mussels. Add at the very end — seafood cooks in 2-3 minutes.</li>
        <li><strong>Vegetarian green curry:</strong> Use firm tofu and extra vegetables. Add a splash of coconut aminos for umami depth.</li>
      </ul>
